    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`. `pytest -q` passes (3 tests), but none of them call `run_tool`: they only use `ToolCallFailed` and the CLI. So the new function hasn't been run against a real session yet.\n\nHow it works:\n\n1. It calls `session.call_tool(name, arguments)`.\n2. If `result.is_error` is true, it raises `ToolCallFailed(name, message)`. The message is the result's text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n3. Otherwise, if `result.structured_content is not None`, it returns that value unchanged. I check for `None` rather than truthiness so that an empty dict still counts as structured output.\n4. Otherwise it returns the text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`. Non-text blocks (images, resources) are skipped.\n\nI used the snake_case field names (`is_error`, `structured_content`) that the installed mcp 2.2.0 uses.\n\nErrors at the protocol level, such as `MCPError`, still pass through unchanged rather than becoming `ToolCallFailed`, because the server sends those as JSON-RPC errors, not as a failed tool result."
